Is Your Approach Optimal?

It has the optimal traversal strategy, but string concatenation creates extra work.

Every expression like:

path + root.val + "->"

creates a new String.

Java String is immutable.

For a long path:

"1->2->3->4->5"

many intermediate strings are created.

So the practical complexity can be roughly:

O(nh + S)

rather than pure O(n).

With LeetCode's small constraints, your solution is perfectly fine.

But we can improve it.

Approach 4 — Optimal: DFS + StringBuilder + Backtracking

This is the cleanest optimized implementation.

Instead of creating a new string on every recursive call, use one mutable:

StringBuilder

We:

Append current value.
If leaf → save current string.
Otherwise append "->".
Recurse.
Remove what we appended.

This is backtracking.

Code
class Solution {

    public List<String> binaryTreePaths(TreeNode root) {

        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null) {
            return result;
        }

        StringBuilder path = new StringBuilder();

        dfs(root, path, result);

        return result;
    }

    private void dfs(
            TreeNode root,
            StringBuilder path,
            List<String> result) {

        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        int lengthBefore = path.length();

        // Add current node
        path.append(root.val);

        // Leaf
        if (root.left == null && root.right == null) {

            result.add(path.toString());

        } else {

            // Add separator
            path.append("->");

            dfs(root.left, path, result);
            dfs(root.right, path, result);
        }

        // Backtrack
        path.setLength(lengthBefore);
    }
}

