Complexity Analysis

Let:

n = number of nodes
h = height
S = total characters in all output paths

We should distinguish traversal complexity from output cost.

Because the problem itself requires returning every path, writing the output costs at least:

Ω(S)
Approach 1 — Copy path at every node
Time  = O(nh + S)
Space = O(nh)

Worst case:

O(n²)
Approach 2 — BFS + String path
Time  = O(nh + S)
Space = O(nh)

because many copied path strings can coexist in the queue.

Approach 3 — Your Recursive String Concatenation
Time  = O(nh + S)
Space = O(h + S)

The O(S) output storage is required.

The recursive traversal stack is:

O(h)
Approach 4 — StringBuilder + Backtracking

Traversal work:

O(n)

Plus the unavoidable output construction:

O(S)

So:

Time = O(n + S)

Auxiliary recursion/path space:

O(h)

excluding the returned result.

This is the cleanest output-sensitive optimal approach.

Comparison Table
Approach	Main Idea	Time	Auxiliary Space
1. Brute	Copy path list at every node	O(nh + S)	O(nh)
2. BFS	Queue stores node + full path	O(nh + S)	O(nh)
3. Your DFS	Recursive String concatenation	O(nh + S)	O(h)
4. DFS + StringBuilder	Mutable path + backtracking	O(n + S)	O(h)
5. DFS + ArrayList backtracking	Store values, stringify at leaves	O(n + S)	O(h)
